A viable native Track B family has emerged from the paper’s Walsh-matrix results: the solver receives a row/column-permuted Walsh Perron similarity and an exact spectrum, and must provide the sparse rational convex combination of Walsh rows that realizes it. The generator plants that combination before forming the spectrum; the checker only recomputes the exact Walsh combination. The standard inverse Walsh–Hadamard transform is efficient and will be disclosed, while the compact route uses singleton bit-mask columns to decode the sparse support. I’m now stress-testing whether that route is genuinely shorter and whether simpler planting-aware heuristics break it.
